  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nWhen the selected option contains a nested `<option selected>`, cloning it into a `<selectedcontent>` never terminates. The clone of the nested option is an option of the `<select>`, so it becomes the selected option, which updates the `<selectedcontent>` again, and so on.\n\n```html\n<select>\n  <button><selectedcontent></selectedcontent></button>\n  <option>X<span><option selected>Y</option></span></option>\n</select>\n```\n\nThe `<span>` is only there so that the parser nests the options. The same tree built with DOM APIs (`createElement`/`append`) loops too, so this is not specific to the parser.\n\n#### Steps\n\n1. The nested `<option selected>Y</option>` has an `option` ancestor, so its [nearest ancestor `select`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-elements.html#option-element-nearest-ancestor-select) is null, and it is not in the select's [list of options](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-elements.html#concept-select-option-list). The only option is the outer one (\"X\"), which the [selectedness setting algorithm](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-elements.html#selectedness-setting-algorithm) selects.\n2. [Update a `selectedcontent`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-elements.html#update-a-selectedcontent) [clones](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-elements.html#clone-an-option-into-a-selectedcontent) the children of the outer option into the `selectedcontent`: `X<span><option selected>Y</option></span>`.\n3. The cloned `<option selected>` has no `option`, `hr` or `datalist` ancestor between itself and the `select` (its ancestors are `span`, `selectedcontent`, `button`, `select`), so it is now in the list of options. Its selectedness is true (it has the `selected` attribute), so it becomes the selected option: \"whenever an option element with its selectedness set to true is added to the select element's list of options, the user agent must set the selectedness of all the other option elements in its list of options to false\".\n4. The selected option changed, so the `selectedcontent` is updated again: the children of the cloned option (\"Y\") replace the content of the `selectedcontent`, which removes the selected clone.\n5. The select lost its selected option, so the selectedness setting algorithm selects the outer option again, and the `selectedcontent` is updated again: back to step 2.\n\nThe [disabled](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-elements.html#selectedcontent-disabled) state of `selectedcontent` already prevents a `selectedcontent` nested in an `option` or in another `selectedcontent` from cloning, but nothing prevents the options cloned into a `selectedcontent` from changing the selection of the select.\n\n#### Chromium\n\nChrome 153.0.8010.12 (Chrome for Testing, headless) hangs on both the markup above and the DOM API version: the load event never fires (or `append()` never returns), the page stops responding, the renderer stays above 100% CPU and its RSS grows to ~9.5 GB within 30 s (~10.3 GB after 3 minutes, still hung).\n\nRelated observation: without the `selected` attribute on the nested option there is no loop, but the clone in the `selectedcontent` is an option of the select (`select.options.length` is 2 and `select.options[0]` is the clone in the `selectedcontent`). Setting the selectedness of the nested option with script (`.selected = true`) instead of the attribute does not loop either, since the selectedness is not cloned.\n\n#### Possible fix\n\nTreat `selectedcontent` like `option`, `hr` and `datalist` in [get the nearest ancestor `select`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-elements.html#option-element-nearest-ancestor-select) (return null), and skip the descendants of `selectedcontent` elements in [get the list of options](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-elements.html#concept-select-option-list). The options in a `selectedcontent` are always clones, so they would no longer be options of the select: cloning could not change the selection, and `select.options` would not include the clones.\n\nSee also #13022 (other side effects of cloning into `selectedcontent`).\n",
